# Source and prose check out with LF on every machine, so two clones of
# this repository hold the same bytes.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Windows scripts keep CRLF. PowerShell in particular is unreliable with
# LF-only files, and these only ever run on Windows.
*.bat text eol=crlf
*.ps1 text eol=crlf

# Evidence is never rewritten, not even by git.
#
# The files under results/ are the instrument's own output. They are CRLF
# because PractRand wrote them on Windows, and the whole point of that
# directory is that the bytes are the ones the run produced. Line-ending
# normalisation is exactly the kind of small, well-meant edit that turns
# a record into a copy of a record -- and this project has already been
# bitten once by CRLF translation, silently destroying a byte stream
# mid-measurement (see rrc/README.md on -std=gnu++14).
#
# -text rather than eol=crlf on purpose: this way whatever was committed
# is what comes back out, including any future file that happens to be
# LF. The rule should protect the record, not enforce a flavour of it.
results/** -text
